WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.732 --> 00:00:10.440
DOMINIC PREUSS: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.600
My name is Dominic Preuss.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.100
I'm the director
and product manager

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.145
for our storage databasing
capacity products

00:00:15.145 --> 00:00:17.040
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.250
So I'm very excited to
be here today, day three

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.520
of this amazing conference, to
talk about database migration

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:23.895
service on GCP.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.792
As we've onboarded more and more
large enterprise customers--

00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:36.500
we've talked about
Target in the keynote.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.880
We've talked about HSBC
and other large companies.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.490
They often have a
dilemma about how

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.630
to get their data
into the cloud.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.450
And so we have a set
of recommendations

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.220
we give to people when they
think about these migrations.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.880
So the first thing is, as
customers are moving their data

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.340
into the cloud, we want to make
sure that they're realizing

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.590
value all the way along.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:58.340
So we want to make
sure that they identify

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:01.850
which workloads to move
first, get on track,

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.350
get some experience,
get comfortable

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.780
moving data data into
the cloud, and then

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.280
that allows you to minimize the
disruption to your business.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.155
So we'll take you through
kind of a framework

00:01:10.155 --> 00:01:12.710
of how we talk about that
and how we think about it.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:14.350
And then I'll bring
up Yair from Alooma

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.874
to talk about how they
do database migrations.

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:21.670
So when we go in and talk to
large customers about database

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.690
migration, we focus on three
phases of the approach.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.620
The first one is assessment.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.370
This is where you actually
look at the data that you have,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.297
think about what would
be most logical to move.

00:01:32.297 --> 00:01:33.880
And then we move
into phase two, which

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.560
is doing the actual migration.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.190
So we'll talk about some
strategies for doing migration.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:39.890
And the last one is validation.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.300
How do you know that your
migration was successful?

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.760
Phase one-- assessment.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.510
The first question is, what
apps and databases need to move?

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.760
One of the things that's
most interesting about my job

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.814
is going into customers.

00:01:53.814 --> 00:01:55.980
And they say, I need to
move some data to the cloud.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:56.930
I say great.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:57.890
What data do you have?

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.240
They're like, I don't know.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.160
So I think, OK, the
first thing we need to do

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.920
is go through and
actually catalog

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.093
and do some discovery on
all of your databases.

00:02:08.093 --> 00:02:09.759
Once we have the full
list of databases,

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.600
we can look at which ones
might be appropriate to move

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.620
to the cloud.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.530
And this might be based on
which databases actually

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:20.430
work well in the cloud, which
ones have managed services.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.890
So you're going to look
at what is the match

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:28.510
or mismatch of your databases to
what's available in the cloud.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:31.600
Once you've identified what
some of those database workloads

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.460
are that might
move to the cloud,

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.350
you need to understand
the capabilities that

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.685
are out there.

00:02:37.685 --> 00:02:40.060
One of the things that we see
is often a really big issue

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.940
is, if you're running older
versions of databases,

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.410
it's often more difficult
to move those to the cloud.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:48.970
If you have an older version
of MySQL or Postgres,

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:51.940
you might not be able to
move that into Cloud SQL,

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.774
because it doesn't
support the same version.

00:02:53.774 --> 00:02:55.481
And so then you might
not be able to take

00:02:55.481 --> 00:02:56.780
advantage of a managed service.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.404
And you might have
to actually put that

00:02:58.404 --> 00:03:00.910
on to a GCE, a Google
Compute Engine VM,

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:02.290
and manage that yourself.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.000
So you have to make sure
that the capabilities that

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.280
are available in the platform
match with the use cases

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.152
that you have.

00:03:08.152 --> 00:03:09.610
Then you have to
figure out what is

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.120
the right path for the cloud.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.720
So if you're moving a
database or an application,

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.630
can that database or
application accept downtime?

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:20.320
Do you have to basically try to
do a live failover migration,

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.260
or can you accept an hour
or two hours of downtime?

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:24.580
Can you do it over a weekend?

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:25.960
Can you do it overnight?

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.080
What are the actual requirements
of that application when you

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.400
want to move it into the cloud?

00:03:33.035 --> 00:03:34.910
And the last one is,
how will does the target

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:36.280
support the application?

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.680
You might want to move
an application that

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.080
has a MySQL database.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:44.830
And that MySQL database might
be supported by Cloud SQL,

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.450
so you can move
that database over.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.130
But for some reason,
that application

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.980
requires something that doesn't
work well in the public cloud.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.020
Maybe it only runs
on bare metal.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.840
Maybe it requires
some APIs or access

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.450
to underlying systems that
make it inappropriate to run

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.020
in the public cloud.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.530
So you need to make sure that
the target of where you want

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.720
to land your
database matches what

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.471
you need from that
application and database.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.500
Phase two is the migration.

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:16.329
What you want to do is
once you've identified

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.820
the applications and workloads
that you want to migrate,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.760
you need to figure out what type
of migration am I going to do.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.440
So this is actually using the
Gartner set of migration types.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.290
And we've actually
added one, because we

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:31.810
think they forgot the most
important one, which is retire.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.640
The first thing you should do
is look at all your databases.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.056
And the first thing
you should say

00:04:35.056 --> 00:04:36.940
is, do I still
need this database?

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:38.110
Are people using it?

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.700
Can it be consolidated?

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.514
Can it be basically retired?

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:42.930
So do I even need
to migrate that?

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:46.370
We think that's a very
important first step.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.080
The second one is replace.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.330
Another thing you need to
do is look at your database.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:54.430
So let's say that you're running
an on-premise CRM solution that

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.030
has a MySQL database.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.050
So your first question is, do
I want to migrate to the cloud,

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.040
or do I just want to
move to a Saas offering?

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.040
Do I just move to Salesforce?

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.310
Do I move to a
fully-hosted solution?

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.110
If I'm moving
Exchange Server, do I

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:09.890
want to go to a hosted
exchange offering?

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:11.860
Do I need to move
those databases at all,

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.780
or can I move to a
full Saas offering?

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.090
That's option number two.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.900
Number three is rehost.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.740
So this is basically
moving that application

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:22.960
and hosting it on GCE VMs.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:25.900
So when you move that
data over, can you

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.090
put it on to a [? Pas ?]
offering, which

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:29.350
is option number four.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:31.192
If I can't, can
I run it on a VM?

00:05:31.192 --> 00:05:33.400
So again, things that end
up being really interesting

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.030
here are really old
versions of databases,

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:38.380
less common databases.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:40.510
So if you have something
like a MarkLogic,

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.850
or if you're running a
Neo4j or something that

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.330
doesn't have a manage offering
today, can you put it on a VM

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:47.450
and manage it yourself?

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.670
And so that's option number
two, which is rehost.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.059
The next one is refactor.

00:05:52.059 --> 00:05:54.600
When I talk about our customers
that are coming to the cloud,

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.680
I find that they break
out into two camps.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:00.104
There's a set of people who
have the right people in-house,

00:06:00.104 --> 00:06:01.770
feel very comfortable
and want to manage

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:03.320
their own infrastructure.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.070
So they want to come,
and they'll actually

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.460
use our IS offerings, our
infrastructures service.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.560
But what we find is
about 80% of people

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.215
are coming to us because
they're having trouble managing.

00:06:13.215 --> 00:06:15.090
They're having trouble
hiring the people that

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:16.756
are necessary to
manage their workloads.

00:06:16.756 --> 00:06:18.330
Or they're having
some other reason

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.580
that they don't want to
manage infrastructure anymore.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.560
So they have a very
strong incentive

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.440
to try to move to our platform
as a service offerings--

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.800
Cloud SQL, our memory
store offering for Redis,

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:29.820
those types of things.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.380
They want to get out of
the business of managing

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.470
their infrastructure.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:34.920
So the question is, can
they move on to our platform

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.670
as a service offering,
another layer up the stack?

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:40.400
The next one is revise.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.940
The first three assume that
you're not making any changes

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:44.960
to the application.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:47.540
You're basically picking
up your application as is,

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.220
running it exactly the same way,
and putting it in the cloud.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.520
The next one is
revise and modernize.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.500
So this is, can you
make some changes

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.540
to your application to take
better advantage of the cloud?

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.750
So an example is I
have an application.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.150
It's currently
running on MySQL 55.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:03.980
That's not offered in Cloud SQL.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:06.605
Can I upgrade that
application to use 56 or 57,

00:07:06.605 --> 00:07:09.020
so now, I can move
that into Cloud SQL?

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.870
Or I have an
internal application.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:14.657
It currently uses Postgres.

00:07:14.657 --> 00:07:16.490
I'm about to go out of
my Postgres instance.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:18.380
I'd really like to
move to Cloud Spanner.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.830
Can I actually re-platform that
and rewrite that application

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.470
to work on top of our
platform as a service?

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.520
And the last one is rebuild.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.250
If I have an application, it
might be long in the tooth.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:30.890
It's missing a bunch
of requirements.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:32.570
I'm just going to rebuild
the whole application.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:34.445
And now, you're in
Greenfield, and you're not

00:07:34.445 --> 00:07:35.300
migrating anything.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:36.740
And now, you can
actually rebuild

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:38.930
using some of our cloud
native technologies,

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:42.980
like Kubernetes, [? SDO ?]
the Cloud Services Platform

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.530
and actually take advantage
of the full power of the cloud

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:48.150
if you actually rewrite the
application from scratch.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:51.170
So these are really
the six different ways.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.180
So it's the five from
Gardner, plus we've

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:56.570
added retire, which we think
is a very useful migration,

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:59.730
for people that are
moving to the cloud.

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:01.230
Then you get to the
validation step,

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:02.610
and this is really important.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.110
Once you've moved
the data, you need

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:07.020
to verify that that
data has come across.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:11.490
Now this might be as simple
as doing a count select star

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:14.022
on all of your tables to
make sure that they're

00:08:14.022 --> 00:08:15.855
the same number of rows
and the old database

00:08:15.855 --> 00:08:17.425
is in the new database.

00:08:17.425 --> 00:08:19.050
Depending on which
database technology,

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.966
you can do check sums
on the data to make sure

00:08:20.966 --> 00:08:22.359
that the data has come across.

00:08:22.359 --> 00:08:24.900
But you really need to make sure
that that data has come over

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.520
wholly.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.540
Then you want to do is
then start to optimize.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.050
So you want to look at, OK,
those MySQL database flags

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.789
that I was using
on-premise, should I

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:34.500
be using the same
flags in the cloud?

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:35.159
Probably not.

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:37.200
You probably want to use
a different set of flags

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.741
to optimize for a
particular workload

00:08:38.741 --> 00:08:41.539
when running in
the public cloud.

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:43.330
We also want to do some
performance checks.

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:45.490
So obviously, once you
have all the data over,

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.110
and you've checked
all your tuning,

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.651
you want to make sure that you
are having an acceptable level

00:08:49.651 --> 00:08:50.530
of performance.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.180
You want to make sure that
something hasn't changed

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.590
with the way that you're hosting
it on-premise, in the cloud.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:56.230
You want to make sure that
your customers are still

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:58.120
happy with the level of
latency and performance

00:08:58.120 --> 00:08:59.744
that you're delivering
from the system.

00:09:03.564 --> 00:09:04.980
So these are the
general framework

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.840
that we think about when we
talk about database migrations.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:11.100
And so how does Google
Cloud Platform help?

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:15.310
So the first thing is
we provide a foundation.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:17.515
All of our services on
Google Cloud Platform

00:09:17.515 --> 00:09:19.390
are industry-leading in
terms of performance,

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:22.570
whether it be our VMs
with persistent disk,

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:25.090
whether it be Cloud
Bigtable or Cloud SQL.

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:27.910
And so we have a
strong foundation

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:32.450
that scales, gives you
industry-leading performance,

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:34.570
and also gives you
industry-leading networking.

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:37.616
And so whenever you are
doing these migrations,

00:09:37.616 --> 00:09:39.490
the thing that ends up
always being the thing

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.620
that you get stuck on
is actually networking.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.960
How do you get network
connectivity from on-premise

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:44.809
into the cloud.

00:09:44.809 --> 00:09:47.350
How do you get in that kind of
activity from your application

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:49.160
into the database.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.760
So all of the tools that we
provide around networking

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:55.762
actually are key enablers of
doing database migrations.

00:09:55.762 --> 00:09:57.970
It also helps when you can
get the bytes there faster

00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:59.640
over the network.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.300
For the capabilities--
a number of features.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:04.230
We're going to talk
about our external master

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:05.680
feature in Cloud SQL.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:08.130
Today, we also have
a number of tools

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:10.080
that we're working
on and then partners.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.150
We spent a lot of time
over the last year, year

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.227
and a half building out
the partner ecosystem.

00:10:14.227 --> 00:10:16.560
So working with partners,
like Alooma and other partners

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.320
in the space, to make sure that
all of those tools that you're

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.900
used to using on-premise
or in other public clouds

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:24.390
work great on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:26.940
And so you can use whatever
tool you're used to using.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:29.364
We don't want to force you
on any particular tool.

00:10:29.364 --> 00:10:31.530
We don't want to tell you
how to do your migrations.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.180
If you have a tool that
you're used to using,

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:34.920
we want to make sure that
we have a great partnership

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.294
with that partner, a great
relationship with that partner

00:10:37.294 --> 00:10:39.300
to make sure that
that tool works.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.190
And the last one is support.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:43.380
So obviously, enterprise--
great support.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:46.650
We now have two different
types of enterprise support.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.890
There's the first, which is
the cost base, where you get

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:51.420
different levels of support.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.878
And then we've gone to a roll
base, where you can basically

00:10:53.878 --> 00:10:55.740
pay for individual
seats of support,

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:57.690
depending on what's
better for your customer.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:59.160
What's better for
you as a customer

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:01.690
and what's better for
your cost structure.

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:05.310
And then lastly, we offer a
number of enterprise support

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:05.970
mechanisms.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:08.490
We have our professional
services organization.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.620
We spent a lot of time
talking about what

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:12.615
the professional
services organization

00:11:12.615 --> 00:11:15.240
that we've built at Google that
can help you with these things.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:19.110
Obviously, the SRE books are a
Site Reliability Engineer team.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:21.900
We published the original site
reliability engineering book,

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:24.610
and we just published the second
book, which is the workbook.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:27.825
And then our CMEs, which are our
Customer Reliability Engineers.

00:11:27.825 --> 00:11:28.950
This is really interesting.

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:30.660
If you are moving
a large application

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:32.760
or if you're launching
a large application,

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:35.940
you can engage with our Customer
Reliability Engineering team.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:39.180
And they will sit down with you
and talk through your launch

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:42.411
plan, your Runbooks, how are
you going to support an outage,

00:11:42.411 --> 00:11:44.910
how are you going to deal with
the various things that could

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:46.354
go wrong when you do a launch.

00:11:46.354 --> 00:11:48.270
So we provide all of
these services for people

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.770
to be able to take
advantage of as they

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:53.531
are migrating that application
or launching new applications.

00:11:57.169 --> 00:11:58.710
We talked a little
bit about tooling.

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:01.584
Now, we're going to talk
about migration to Cloud SQL.

00:12:01.584 --> 00:12:03.000
One of the most
common patterns we

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.958
see when people are moving
relational databases

00:12:04.958 --> 00:12:07.980
to the cloud is this model
where, basically, you

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:11.520
have a MySQL database
that is managed somewhere.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:12.600
This might be on-premise.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.200
It might be on
another public cloud.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:15.690
But that's where your master is.

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:18.190
And so the easiest way to get
your data into Google Cloud

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:25.200
Platform is to add a replica
of that database in Cloud SQL.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:29.940
So in this case, for
Cloud SQL for MySQL,

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:33.360
basically, you can add
a Cloud SQL replica

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:37.470
that is speaking to a master
instance, a master node that

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:38.620
is somewhere else.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:41.400
So this way, you can
replicate all of your data

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:44.920
into Google Cloud Platform using
MySQL's native replication.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:47.100
So you get all of the
consistency guarantees

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.770
and all the things that
are possible through MySQL

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.410
via asynchronous
replication, so you're not

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:55.890
impacting your performance, to
get your data into the cloud.

00:12:55.890 --> 00:13:00.690
Once it's into the cloud, you
can then add a failover replica

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:02.040
so that you have HA.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:04.530
And then you can basically
trigger a failover

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:09.420
to then move the application
from speaking to your master

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.360
instance that is somewhere else
to the Cloud SQL replica, which

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:15.270
was a read-only replica, which
is now your master replica.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.060
And you have HA by
having a second replica

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:20.010
where that data is managed.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:23.790
So now, you've moved all of
your data from another location

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:25.440
into Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.870
You've now replicated it so
that it's highly available.

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.700
And now, you can
repoint your application

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.200
to take advantage
of that new setup.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:39.110
One of the things that
we advocate always

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:42.579
whenever we're doing a migration
of an application or database

00:13:42.579 --> 00:13:44.870
is that we really encourage
people to take the simplest

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:46.200
possible approach.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:48.260
It's very easy to
boil the ocean when

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:49.580
you're trying to move data.

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:51.038
So it's very
important that you try

00:13:51.038 --> 00:13:54.810
to start with the simplest
possible [? implementation. ?]

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:58.140
The vast majority of databases--
usually this is around 80%

00:13:58.140 --> 00:13:59.700
of all of the databases--

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.790
you can move very simply
with some downtime.

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:07.740
Basically, you have an
existing database instance.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:11.160
You stop writes
to that database.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:12.780
Reads can continue to come in.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:14.080
You export that data.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:16.710
So if it's MySQL, it's a SQL
[? dom. ?] It could be CSV.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:18.350
It could be whatever
data format.

00:14:18.350 --> 00:14:21.570
You copy that data into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:23.340
And then, using Cloud
SQL or whichever

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:25.890
database you're using,
import that data in, and then

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:27.480
move the database across.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:30.960
So this is by far the simplest
way of moving your data across.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:32.812
Now this might be
an hour of downtime.

00:14:32.812 --> 00:14:34.770
It might be two hours,
depending how much data.

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.560
But if you're dealing with
hundred gigabytes of data,

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.500
smaller data sets, then this
is a very acceptable way

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:41.400
of doing this.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:44.980
If your application can be down
overnight or over the weekend,

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:47.695
this is by far the simplest
way to do a database migration.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:56.204
Now the key factor in
that migration scenario

00:14:56.204 --> 00:14:57.870
that I just gave you
is how long does it

00:14:57.870 --> 00:15:01.590
take to take that import and
put it into the new thing?

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:05.500
So if you are copying
the data-- you've

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:07.510
done an export of your
database-- copying it

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:09.635
into Google Cloud Storage
because our network tends

00:15:09.635 --> 00:15:11.230
to be very fast.

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:13.720
The long pole of this
process is basically

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:17.650
how long it takes to get your
data from Google Cloud Storage

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:19.770
into your target
database, whether that be

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:22.550
Cloud SQL or Cassandra,
whatever it happens to be.

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:24.670
So here's some
general best practices

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:27.860
that we try to explain to people
when importing database data.

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:31.060
So the first thing is,
when you import your data,

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:33.190
don't include your indexes.

00:15:33.190 --> 00:15:34.300
Bring your data across.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:35.140
Bring the raw data.

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:37.330
Don't try to bring over
all your secondary indexes

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:38.770
at the same time.

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:41.950
Import your data first and then
add your secondary indexes.

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:43.840
This dramatically
lowers the amount

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:46.351
of import time for
most databases.

00:15:46.351 --> 00:15:48.100
The second thing is
do your best to import

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:50.205
the data in primary key order.

00:15:50.205 --> 00:15:51.580
Try to make sure
that your export

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.165
is ordered by primary key.

00:15:53.165 --> 00:15:54.790
So that when you're
importing the data,

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:56.740
it makes it as easy as
possible for the database

00:15:56.740 --> 00:15:57.573
to insert that data.

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:04.850
If you are using
MySQL or Postgres--

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:06.590
and MySQL specifically--

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:09.320
it's really important that you
select a instance type that

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:12.960
has as much RAM as at
least your largest table,

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:15.170
if not your entire dataset.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:18.759
By bringing all that data in on
a really large memory instance,

00:16:18.759 --> 00:16:20.800
you basically can keep
all of the data in memory.

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:22.841
And it makes it much easier
to write the data in.

00:16:22.841 --> 00:16:25.460
So use a much larger memory
size instance initially.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:27.080
Then you might need
to run production.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:28.705
You can always
downsize it later.

00:16:28.705 --> 00:16:30.830
And so this will really
speed up the import process

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:32.300
and decrease the
downtime that you

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:34.820
have in the database migration.

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:38.150
The second thing is
use our SSD storage.

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:40.850
Persistent disk, our
block storage device,

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:42.860
comes in two flavors--

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:44.750
magnetic disk and solid state.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:48.830
And so if you're importing into
a GCE VM or into Cloud SQL,

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:51.590
using this option will
dramatically speed up

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:54.935
your imports into the system.

00:16:54.935 --> 00:16:56.310
Another thing to
remember is when

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:58.650
you're using persistent
disk, the amount

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:01.080
of IOPS you get on your disk
are dependent to the size

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:01.920
of the disks.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:05.407
So make sure you use at
least a 500 gigabyte disk

00:17:05.407 --> 00:17:07.990
to make sure you're getting the
maximum throughput on that VM.

00:17:10.714 --> 00:17:12.380
And lastly, make sure
you're monitoring.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:14.240
You can watch things
like IOPS, whether this

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:19.410
be from your GCE VM or if this
is in your Cloud SQL instance.

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:22.256
You can do this via the cloud
console or via Stackdriver.

00:17:22.256 --> 00:17:24.589
And then obviously, keep an
eye on your read operations.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:26.385
Because very high
read operations

00:17:26.385 --> 00:17:27.760
might indicate
that your database

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:30.667
is paging, which will basically
increase your import time.

00:17:30.667 --> 00:17:32.750
So these are all things
to think about when you're

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:35.041
importing the data to try to
minimize the downtime when

00:17:35.041 --> 00:17:38.096
you're migrating.

00:17:38.096 --> 00:17:39.470
And then after
you import, again,

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:41.136
because you're in the
cloud, you haven't

00:17:41.136 --> 00:17:43.250
bought a physical device
that you can't resize,

00:17:43.250 --> 00:17:46.280
you can resize your database,
make it bigger or smaller.

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:49.139
You can turn on the binary
logging as necessary.

00:17:49.139 --> 00:17:50.930
Turning this off ahead
of time in Cloud SQL

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:53.360
will dramatically increase
your import times.

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.460
And then add your
indexes after the fact.

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:57.710
Don't add indexes when you
are importing the data.

00:17:57.710 --> 00:17:59.070
Add them after the fact.

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:00.920
It's much, much faster and will
save you a tremendous amount

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:01.545
of import time.

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.850
Now if you're moving
an application that

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:08.560
can't accept
downtime, you're going

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:10.720
to want to look at using
replication to actually

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:13.330
move your data across.

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:15.112
As we talked about
here, you're actually

00:18:15.112 --> 00:18:16.070
moving the data across.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.986
We're going to replace
that step with actually

00:18:17.986 --> 00:18:18.885
doing replication.

00:18:18.885 --> 00:18:20.260
Now if you're
doing like-to-like,

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:23.080
if you're doing MySQL to MySQL,
Postgres to Postgres, Cassandra

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:26.320
to Cassandra, you can basically
use the native replication.

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.830
If you're doing something
like MySQL to Postgres,

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:31.912
or you're doing a
heterogeneous migration, then

00:18:31.912 --> 00:18:33.620
some of the tools,
like Attunity, Alooma,

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:35.740
some of the other partners
can help you really

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:36.910
with that workload.

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:39.100
Because they can
provide you replication

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:40.957
that isn't native replication.

00:18:40.957 --> 00:18:43.290
So the idea here is that
you're replicating all the data

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:44.670
across.

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:47.790
And then once the new
instance has caught up,

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:50.407
and you know that the
lag is sufficient--

00:18:50.407 --> 00:18:51.990
if this can't ever
catch up, you might

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:54.870
have to stop writes for
a small amount of time

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:56.560
while that replication
catches up.

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:58.680
But assuming you can
get to a steady state

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.330
where there's very
little lag, then

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:02.610
you basically can move your
application over and have

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:04.320
it speak to the other instance.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:09.482
And therefore, you can have
very low downtime migrations.

00:19:09.482 --> 00:19:11.190
Another workload that
we talk about a lot

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:14.130
is migrating Cassandra to Cloud
Bigtable or Cloud Spanner.

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:16.830
For this talk, we're going
to focus on Bigtable mostly

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:19.830
because there's lots of Cloud
Spanner sessions talking

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.800
about migration.

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:25.800
One of the most common use
cases we see for Cloud Bigtable

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:28.470
is people wanting to
migrate off of their H base

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:29.910
of their Cassandra clusters.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:31.800
So we see this as a
very common use case

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:34.470
for people moving
to Cloud Bigtable.

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:37.274
One of the big questions is,
if I'm coming off of Cassandra,

00:19:37.274 --> 00:19:39.690
should I use Cloud Bigtable
or should I use Cloud Spanner?

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:41.790
So we put together a
little bit of information

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:43.320
to help you choose
between the two.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:47.680
Because Cassandra has a very
wide variety of use cases,

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:50.280
and so how you're
using Cassandra

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:52.295
will determine which
cloud service you want.

00:19:52.295 --> 00:19:53.670
So you want to
use Cloud Bigtable

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:55.461
if you're serving
derived data or if you're

00:19:55.461 --> 00:19:57.512
doing batch ingested analytics.

00:19:57.512 --> 00:19:59.470
If you're using it as a
true wide column store,

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:03.330
and you're trying to write data
in and use it as an analytics

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:04.830
cluster, then Cloud
Bigtable's going

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.930
to be a better solution for you.

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:09.826
If you're serving user
visible and mutatable data,

00:20:09.826 --> 00:20:11.700
especially if you have
multi-row transactions

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:13.680
or you need secondary
indexes, then you're

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:15.441
going to want to
use Cloud Spanner.

00:20:15.441 --> 00:20:16.940
A way to think about
this is, if you

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:21.270
are using Cassandra
with the local quorum

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:23.550
to write to a
single zone cluster

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:25.657
with eventual consistency,
then Cloud Bigtable

00:20:25.657 --> 00:20:27.240
is probably the right
solution for you

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:30.720
and will give you cost
advantage over Cloud Spanner.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.850
If you're using Spanner with
each quorum or local quorums,

00:20:33.850 --> 00:20:36.912
but it spans multiple
regions in the ring,

00:20:36.912 --> 00:20:39.120
then you're going to want
to use Cloud Spanner to get

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:40.772
the same level of functionality.

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:45.185
So how do we do this?

00:20:45.185 --> 00:20:47.560
You have an application that's
speaking to your Cassandra

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.050
cluster.

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:52.600
The most common way that
we see customers migrating

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.837
and what we recommend is what
we call a dual write structure.

00:20:55.837 --> 00:20:57.670
So basically, you have
an application that's

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.950
writing to some sort of queue.

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:01.120
This could be a Kafka queue.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:03.000
It could be RabbitMQ.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:05.860
It might be some place on your
application where you hold.

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:08.620
And then basically, we recommend
that you do tool writes.

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:11.290
So you have two consumers
of that queue that

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:16.044
write the data to both Cassandra
and either Bigtable or Spanner.

00:21:16.044 --> 00:21:18.460
So what this does is it allows
you to basically write data

00:21:18.460 --> 00:21:20.950
to both databases while
the application is

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:23.350
talking to Cassandra.

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:25.870
Once you've gotten a
sufficient amount of data

00:21:25.870 --> 00:21:31.310
into the Cassandra cluster
and it is caught up,

00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:37.070
then you can look at moving it
into and switching the path.

00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:40.000
So here, if you don't want
to run your own consumers,

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:41.460
you could use Cloud Data Flow.

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:43.054
You use Dataproc to do this.

00:21:43.054 --> 00:21:44.470
You could also use
Cloud Functions

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:49.370
or some of our other
serverless architectures.

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:51.050
We talked about Kafka.

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:55.820
Some suggestions-- you can use
Apache Kafka if your writes

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:59.992
are non-idempotent.

00:21:59.992 --> 00:22:01.200
I always get this word wrong.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.810
This is one of the most
difficult words for me to say.

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:06.390
If you need in-order message
delivering is required

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:08.790
or if you need compatibility
with the existing Kafka

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:11.480
tool, Kafka [? Screen, ?] or
whatever it happens to be.

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:16.720
You can use Cloud Data Flow if
your writes are idempotent--

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:19.860
try that again-- or out of
order message delivery is OK

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.727
or it needs to be
globally replicated.

00:22:25.412 --> 00:22:27.620
So then once you've gotten
all the data written over,

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:29.780
you've done an export to
kind of get the bulk data,

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:31.574
and then you've done
a written, now, you

00:22:31.574 --> 00:22:32.990
can actually move
your application

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:36.722
to speak to Cloud Bigtable and
start to read the applications.

00:22:36.722 --> 00:22:38.430
You're still doing
writes to both places,

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:39.450
so you can roll back.

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:41.991
But now, you can read the data
from your new database source.

00:22:46.616 --> 00:22:47.990
Then you're mission
accomplished.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:50.479
The migration is done.

00:22:50.479 --> 00:22:52.020
All right, so with
that, I would like

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:54.061
to bring up somebody who
has much more experience

00:22:54.061 --> 00:22:55.532
than I do at
database migrations.

00:22:55.532 --> 00:22:57.240
So I'll bring up Yair
Weinberger, the CTO

00:22:57.240 --> 00:22:59.488
and co-founder of Alooma
to share some of the tools

00:22:59.488 --> 00:23:00.237
that they provide.

00:23:00.237 --> 00:23:04.710
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:06.210
YAIR WEINBERGER:
Thank you, Dominic.

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:07.740
Can you hear me?

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:10.310
My mic all right?

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:12.440
So I'm Yair, CTO of
Alooma, as I mentioned.

00:23:12.440 --> 00:23:14.940
And we deal with
migrations all the time.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:18.110
And a lot of it is
to [? GCP ?] as well.

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:19.790
And I'm going to talk
a little bit more

00:23:19.790 --> 00:23:21.260
in depth about
database migration,

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:25.040
especially the more complex use
cases and things you can enter.

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:28.235
But before that, just a little
bit about generally Alooma

00:23:28.235 --> 00:23:30.710
and data migration.

00:23:30.710 --> 00:23:33.370
I'm sure this is not
news to any one of you.

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:34.310
We used to be on-prem.

00:23:34.310 --> 00:23:36.140
We used to have our
compute on-prem.

00:23:36.140 --> 00:23:39.530
We use to have our storage
on-prem, our database on-prem.

00:23:39.530 --> 00:23:42.800
And what happens now
is we want to move.

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:44.500
We want to move to
modern technology.

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:46.447
We want to manage the
service in the cloud.

00:23:46.447 --> 00:23:48.530
We want to use the best
of [? free ?] technologies

00:23:48.530 --> 00:23:51.140
for every one of our
workloads, our applications

00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:52.609
are moving to containers.

00:23:52.609 --> 00:23:54.650
Our storage is moving to
the cloud storage, which

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:56.590
is infinite and much cheaper.

00:23:56.590 --> 00:24:01.610
And then our databases are also
moving to the cloud databases.

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:04.190
And this is what
Alooma is about.

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:08.560
And what I didn't mention is
that new applications are just

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:09.310
born in the cloud.

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:10.520
It's very easy.

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:12.800
We create it inside a container.

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:16.370
We use Cloud SQL, and
we just let it fly,

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:18.140
and it's just working
out-of-the-box.

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:21.380
But whenever we are looking at
a little bit bigger company,

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:23.234
it has a lot of
legacy applications.

00:24:23.234 --> 00:24:24.650
Some of them, the
people who wrote

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:31.470
the code don't exist anymore
or don't work for the company.

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:36.060
Some of them has a lot of legacy
databases, petabyte of data.

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:37.720
I think one of the
[? fanfics ?] was

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:41.540
how to move over three petabytes
of data only to be moved.

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:43.580
This is that dragon
need to be tamed.

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:46.940
And everyone who experienced
that knows it can be extremely

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:48.500
difficult and time--consuming.

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:50.070
Sometimes even takes years.

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:54.320
So just saying I want
to move to the cloud

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:57.710
can be quite difficult.

00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:00.150
When migrating, there are
three types of migration.

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:02.050
And I'm going to focus
on the data migration

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:05.300
part, which is what Alooma
is focused on as well.

00:25:05.300 --> 00:25:06.610
There is the compute instances.

00:25:06.610 --> 00:25:08.800
We want to move our
compute and workload

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:11.590
to the cloud, our
applications and services,

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:13.760
and the data itself.

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:16.570
Now because this
process is not atomic,

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:19.120
it's not like we
can start on-prem,

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:21.420
and then the next day, we
are fully in the cloud.

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:22.360
It takes time.

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:23.210
It can take months.

00:25:23.210 --> 00:25:24.640
It can takes years.

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:27.670
While we make that move, not
only we want to move the data,

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:30.100
but we want to keep the
two environments in sync.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:32.350
We want to make sure
whatever we have on-prem

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:35.260
can be synced to whatever we
have in the cloud and sometimes

00:25:35.260 --> 00:25:37.390
even back, sometimes
even vice versa.

00:25:40.510 --> 00:25:42.460
And that's where
Alooma comes in.

00:25:42.460 --> 00:25:47.790
Alooma have built the tooling
and the product as a service

00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:51.330
in the cloud to migrate out
of the on-premise data from

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:55.170
databases, from storage, from
data centers, enterprise,

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:59.040
data warehouses into the cloud--

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:02.110
actually all clouds, but
Google in that context.

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:08.810
So how does a migration
data pipeline look like?

00:26:08.810 --> 00:26:11.650
This is a little bit of
a peek under the hood.

00:26:11.650 --> 00:26:13.480
You need to have a
lot of connectors,

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:14.350
connect to anything.

00:26:14.350 --> 00:26:17.290
And that could be RDBMS, which
is the focus of this talk today

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:20.420
and what I am going to show
soon how we migrate a database

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:21.060
instance.

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:24.700
But that also could be files
that can exist on FTP servers,

00:26:24.700 --> 00:26:27.310
on servers, on some
cloud storage that we

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:28.410
might have put in there.

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:32.200
That could be event streams
are coming from mobile devices,

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:33.567
coming from applications.

00:26:33.567 --> 00:26:35.650
And they can even be the
Saas vendors that we use.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:39.640
Because already, not all of our
application are inside the org.

00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:41.050
Some of them we use Salesforce.

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:42.133
We use [? Marketo ?] hubs.

00:26:42.133 --> 00:26:43.660
But all of those
new Saas vendors,

00:26:43.660 --> 00:26:46.840
we want to make sure we are able
to read their data as well, so

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:48.460
a lot of connectors.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:53.069
And then once we get
all of that data in,

00:26:53.069 --> 00:26:54.860
we, of course, put it
in the message queue.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:56.669
Alooma, under the
hood, uses Kafka.

00:26:56.669 --> 00:26:58.210
But that's, of
course, non-mandatory.

00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:01.240
We do believe it is
currently one of the best

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:02.800
options in the market.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:04.720
We want to be able to
transform or modify

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:05.914
the data that goes through.

00:27:05.914 --> 00:27:07.580
And that's what we
call the code engine.

00:27:07.580 --> 00:27:09.490
They're built to run
code on the data that

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:10.900
goes through the stream.

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:13.630
And we want to be able to
know how the data looks like,

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:14.860
what is the schema.

00:27:14.860 --> 00:27:18.730
So again, I'm sure many of you
have used Hadoop in the past

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:22.600
and are familiar with the schema
on read concept, where we just

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:25.060
push everything
into that data lake

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:30.040
and then take care of the schema
when we want to read the data.

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:33.460
I believe that this has
created a lot of problem.

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:36.690
Because sometimes, the
schema changes over time.

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:40.037
The schema is [? askew. ?] And
if we only find out about it

00:27:40.037 --> 00:27:42.370
when we try to read the data,
we might be in big trouble

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:43.120
already.

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:45.930
So we need to understand
and maintain the schema

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:47.280
or how the data look like.

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:50.680
And it can be either
imported from the source

00:27:50.680 --> 00:27:52.240
or inferred from
the data itself.

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:56.110
So if we read data from Oracle
database or anything that

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:57.790
has a schema with
it, that schema

00:27:57.790 --> 00:27:59.560
can be imported and translated.

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:02.770
If we're reading data from
files or from event streams,

00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:07.622
sometimes the schema needs to be
inferred from the data itself.

00:28:07.622 --> 00:28:09.830
And then we need to make
sure we know to put the data

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:12.330
wherever it belongs.

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:16.560
So I want to show you a little
bit lift-and-shift use case.

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:23.930
Can I get the other screen?

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:24.770
Great.

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:26.580
So this is the Alooma product.

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:31.280
And what I want to show you
is how we take database MySQL

00:28:31.280 --> 00:28:32.870
on-prem--

00:28:32.870 --> 00:28:35.000
looks like I will
need my VPN here.

00:28:37.550 --> 00:28:38.890
Sorry about that.

00:28:38.890 --> 00:28:42.400
And take that and move
it to a MySQL Cloud SQL

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:43.937
instance in the cloud.

00:28:43.937 --> 00:28:46.270
And I actually want to add a
little bit of a twist here.

00:28:46.270 --> 00:28:48.730
Because as Dominic
said, sometimes there

00:28:48.730 --> 00:28:51.760
are just very simple
migration, where we can just

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:54.610
enable replication
between the master on-prem

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:57.190
and whatever the Cloud
SQL we have in the cloud

00:28:57.190 --> 00:29:00.560
and let the MySQL
application take care of it.

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:04.210
But I want to introduce
another very common concept

00:29:04.210 --> 00:29:05.170
of sharding.

00:29:05.170 --> 00:29:07.860
My on-premise
databases is sharded.

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:09.730
And I'm writing to
two different shards.

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:12.070
But there is no need
to keep the sharding

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:13.314
when I'm moving to Cloud SQL.

00:29:13.314 --> 00:29:14.980
When I'm moving to
Cloud SQL, of course,

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:17.300
I can make the
instances much bigger.

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:20.000
I already have highly
available out of the box.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:22.840
So I want to data sharding.

00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:25.570
And I want to write everything
to the same database instance.

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:27.520
It makes my applications
more maintainable.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:29.800
It makes my querying the
data more maintainable,

00:29:29.800 --> 00:29:32.320
because I don't need to
query for multiple shards.

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:34.060
So there is a little
bit of a twist.

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:36.100
If I just enable
replication as is,

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:38.690
I will keep the
sharding in place.

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:40.280
So what do I do here?

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:48.160
I started my Cloud SQL instance
of MySQL, which is here.

00:29:48.160 --> 00:29:50.510
And I configure it as
a target for Alooma.

00:29:50.510 --> 00:29:55.070
So very simple, I configure
the same cloud SQL

00:29:55.070 --> 00:29:57.980
as the target for Alooma.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:01.640
And then, that I already did,
but the next thing I need to do

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:05.240
is just to add the MySQL
data source or sources.

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:06.920
So this is, again, very simple.

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:08.630
And, by the way,
that can be Oracle.

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:12.860
That can be pretty much any
cloud database, SQL Server,

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:13.610
Postgres.

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:16.700
So I can also do lift and
shift or lift and modernize

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:18.710
the same way.

00:30:18.710 --> 00:30:22.690
In that case, if I take MySQL,
then all I need to provide

00:30:22.690 --> 00:30:26.410
is the connection details,
the type of replication.

00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:27.820
Of course,
[INAUDIBLE] to capture

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:30.160
is the most recommended one.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:32.800
But it can be incremental
application or full dump

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:37.000
load replication as well and
the list of tables to replicate.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:38.770
Of course, I can
leave that empty.

00:30:38.770 --> 00:30:41.741
And then it just means I'm
shifting my entire database.

00:30:41.741 --> 00:30:43.990
I don't want to just move a
specific subset of tables.

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:50.410
Once I got that done,
once that is configured,

00:30:50.410 --> 00:30:53.220
I have my data
running and streaming.

00:30:53.220 --> 00:30:54.730
I can click on it.

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:58.110
I can see live samples, whatever
comes through the stream.

00:30:58.110 --> 00:31:02.820
So in that case, I've made
a very simple use case.

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:04.410
I have my on-prem database.

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:07.200
I have two shards, which
are in one table, which

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:09.030
is transactions.

00:31:09.030 --> 00:31:12.810
And every shard, there's
only one row in it.

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:15.540
Of course, in reality,
it's much bigger than that.

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:18.570
Once I set up that
connection, what

00:31:18.570 --> 00:31:20.040
Alooma will do
under the hood is it

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:24.060
will start replicating the
ongoing changes over time.

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:28.260
And it will create a process
to dump the entire dataset.

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:31.500
And then, no matter if it's
one gigabyte, 10 gigabyte, 100

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:34.126
gigabyte or a
terabyte, that process

00:31:34.126 --> 00:31:35.250
will happen under the hood.

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:39.780
It will be optimized to migrate
the data as best as possible.

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:41.760
Take into consideration
the other thing Dominic

00:31:41.760 --> 00:31:43.640
mentioned earlier
about re-integrating

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:46.940
that says to import by the
order of primary key, et cetera.

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:49.290
So all of these, you're
already getting that

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:52.390
immediately out of the box.

00:31:52.390 --> 00:31:54.140
And then what I also
set up is I just

00:31:54.140 --> 00:31:57.380
set up a process that
continuously updates the amount

00:31:57.380 --> 00:31:58.580
on my own two orders.

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:07.880
So as I mentioned, we do
want to take those two shards

00:32:07.880 --> 00:32:09.710
and put them into one table.

00:32:09.710 --> 00:32:12.759
But those shards can have
conflicting primary keys.

00:32:12.759 --> 00:32:15.050
Those shards can have the
same primary key in shard one

00:32:15.050 --> 00:32:16.190
and shard two.

00:32:16.190 --> 00:32:18.920
So what we do is here, we
leverage the Alooma code engine

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:19.845
that I mentioned.

00:32:19.845 --> 00:32:22.220
And we change the data as it
is going through the stream.

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:25.730
And that's why we couldn't
use just simple replication.

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:28.070
And it's really a
one line module.

00:32:28.070 --> 00:32:31.340
All it does is it takes
the shard from the metadata

00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:35.480
that Alooma attached to the data
and put it in the event itself.

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:37.755
So I can actually get live
samples from the data.

00:32:37.755 --> 00:32:40.715
In the metadata I have, this
is, like, shard number two.

00:32:40.715 --> 00:32:42.980
And now, I'm testing it.

00:32:42.980 --> 00:32:47.480
I'm just adding a shard ID
field to the data itself.

00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:51.170
And that shard ID, I can
see was added to the data.

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:58.570
So on-prem, we've
seen the data just

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:01.160
has ID created
[? and the amount field. ?]

00:33:01.160 --> 00:33:05.102
On the Cloud SQL part, we
do have the shard as well.

00:33:05.102 --> 00:33:06.560
So we have shard
one and shard two.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:07.976
And we have data
from both shards.

00:33:07.976 --> 00:33:09.789
And they don't
overwrite each other,

00:33:09.789 --> 00:33:11.330
because the shard
is considered to be

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:15.680
as part of the new
primary key on the target.

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:17.300
All right, so we got that going.

00:33:17.300 --> 00:33:18.950
It's replicating continuously.

00:33:18.950 --> 00:33:20.280
It's very nice.

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:22.520
Now I'm moving my application.

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:24.470
Unfortunately,
again, it's not very

00:33:24.470 --> 00:33:26.670
simple to move the application.

00:33:26.670 --> 00:33:29.360
Sometimes I cannot move
the application at once.

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:31.460
I need to move the
application in parts.

00:33:31.460 --> 00:33:34.010
So what I did was
I moved the part

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:36.560
that only writes to
one of the shards.

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:39.186
So I'm going to change it.

00:33:39.186 --> 00:33:40.810
So right now, I'm
updating both shards.

00:33:40.810 --> 00:33:44.470
That's my demo application,
which is updating the shards.

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:47.620
Of course, that is
now disconnected.

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:49.360
Let's see if I can
restore it quickly.

00:33:54.800 --> 00:33:59.510
This is a real-life demo.

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:02.370
All right, so I was
updating both shards.

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:05.010
Now, I'm only going to
update shard number one.

00:34:05.010 --> 00:34:08.219
Because I migrated the
application from shard two,

00:34:08.219 --> 00:34:10.889
it only working
directly with Cloud SQL.

00:34:10.889 --> 00:34:13.497
So it no longer updates
my on-prem database.

00:34:13.497 --> 00:34:16.080
And the only thing that's being
updated in my on-prem database

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:18.010
is shard number one.

00:34:18.010 --> 00:34:22.620
However, I also want to make
sure that I'm not wrong.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:24.752
So I want to make
sure I didn't mess up.

00:34:24.752 --> 00:34:26.460
I want to make sure
that there is nothing

00:34:26.460 --> 00:34:29.010
left in that
hodgepodge of servers

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:32.130
that I have on-prem that's still
writing to shard two on-prem.

00:34:32.130 --> 00:34:34.739
I want to be confident and
validate that I actually

00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:36.820
did the migration successfully.

00:34:36.820 --> 00:34:39.600
So what I'm going to
do is I'm going just

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:43.260
to add another small
cotangent piece in the model

00:34:43.260 --> 00:34:43.949
that I created.

00:34:43.949 --> 00:34:47.400
I'm just going to commentate
that out, which says, OK,

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:50.940
if I'm reading data
from shard number two,

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:52.650
let's raise an exception.

00:34:52.650 --> 00:34:54.945
Let's say, OK, I'm
not allowing anymore

00:34:54.945 --> 00:34:56.320
[? outage ?] on
shard number two.

00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:57.900
That will give me two things.

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:00.450
One, it will give me the
visibility that it happened.

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:02.940
But it will also stop
the data from moving,

00:35:02.940 --> 00:35:05.040
because I probably don't
want that data moving.

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:06.420
Now I can also move that later.

00:35:06.420 --> 00:35:09.780
But for now, I want to
stop it from moving.

00:35:09.780 --> 00:35:11.720
Let's just make sure
that it's working.

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:13.680
We've got here shard number two.

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:16.410
And now, when we run tests,
we see that updates on-prem

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:17.530
are no longer expected.

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:22.420
So that's where we don't want
that data to move anymore.

00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:25.360
I'm going to deploy my code.

00:35:25.360 --> 00:35:27.360
And now, it's still all
fine, because we are not

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:29.940
moving any data to
shard two because we

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:31.560
changed the application.

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:34.330
Now let's simulate
our rogue application,

00:35:34.330 --> 00:35:38.200
the one that we forgot in
the data center that is going

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:40.942
to update shard number two.

00:35:40.942 --> 00:35:42.400
What I'm going do
here is I'm going

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:45.280
to insert a new transaction
to the on-prem databases

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:48.595
for shard number two, which
is not supposed to happen.

00:35:48.595 --> 00:35:50.220
And then we'll see
how we deal with it.

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:57.180
That's what I did.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:36:00.430
And while that is populating,
I actually want to show you

00:36:00.430 --> 00:36:04.640
another nice element, which is
a live visualization of the data

00:36:04.640 --> 00:36:05.780
stream itself.

00:36:05.780 --> 00:36:07.820
So part of the Alooma
product is the ability

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:10.790
to visualize everything that's
coming in from the data stream

00:36:10.790 --> 00:36:11.570
live.

00:36:11.570 --> 00:36:12.680
So we can see the source.

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:14.630
You can filter on
the data itself.

00:36:14.630 --> 00:36:16.710
You can even see the
events themselves.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:19.130
And this is looking nice
and also very useful

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:21.830
for [? gut checks. ?] Now
this is a more high volume

00:36:21.830 --> 00:36:24.890
system that's actually
storing data in live.

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:28.100
I can filter on only events that
are coming from IOS or mobile,

00:36:28.100 --> 00:36:29.220
for example.

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:32.885
I can actually see those events.

00:36:32.885 --> 00:36:36.440
Except being a nice eye
candy, it's also very useful

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:38.180
just for [? a gut check. ?]
Am I getting

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:39.680
what I'm expecting to get?

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:43.480
Is that data really make sense?

00:36:43.480 --> 00:36:45.550
So going back to our
rogue application

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:57.440
that updated the shard, by now,
we have already read that data.

00:37:01.190 --> 00:37:04.790
And what you can see is we
have wrong data in what you

00:37:04.790 --> 00:37:05.960
call the restream queue.

00:37:05.960 --> 00:37:08.150
So this is where
we handle errors.

00:37:08.150 --> 00:37:10.560
We can see what happened.

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:12.020
You got an event.

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:13.940
That event says shard two.

00:37:13.940 --> 00:37:15.650
That's not supposed to happen.

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:16.752
We did something bad.

00:37:16.752 --> 00:37:17.960
We still have an application.

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:21.260
We need to find out where it
is, which server does it sit in,

00:37:21.260 --> 00:37:25.040
why is it writing those events
into my old on-prem database.

00:37:25.040 --> 00:37:27.720
So that's part of
our validation step.

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:29.940
And I can actually find out
exactly which application

00:37:29.940 --> 00:37:31.590
that is, what is it writing.

00:37:31.590 --> 00:37:32.800
It makes it very easy for me.

00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:34.300
In fact, I actually
have the sample.

00:37:34.300 --> 00:37:38.262
And the data itself makes it
very easy for me to find out.

00:37:38.262 --> 00:37:39.720
And once I'm
satisfied with it, I'm

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:43.590
just going to say, OK, I
found that application.

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:45.840
I can either purge
the data back,

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:49.380
or I can just purge all of
this data and throw it away.

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:51.390
I'm saying, all right,
now, I've successfully

00:37:51.390 --> 00:37:52.590
migrated that shard.

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:54.550
I can move on to migrate
the other shard well.

00:38:00.550 --> 00:38:02.460
So this was a very
simple use case

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:05.040
of lift-and-shift
migration, of taking data

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:06.760
from MySQL and the source.

00:38:06.760 --> 00:38:10.309
And again, we already
saw a couple of snags

00:38:10.309 --> 00:38:12.600
that you can hit while only
doing that very simple lift

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:13.830
and shift.

00:38:13.830 --> 00:38:16.560
Again, when you're moving
your real enterprise data set,

00:38:16.560 --> 00:38:20.790
it has thousands of tables
with terabytes of data.

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:23.050
That, of course, can
be much more complex.

00:38:23.050 --> 00:38:25.050
And then once you do that,
it's also very simple

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:27.720
to do life and modernize
instead of lift and shift.

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:29.630
So I will take that MySQL.

00:38:29.630 --> 00:38:32.940
I just replace it with
Spanner exactly the same say.

00:38:32.940 --> 00:38:34.650
Even I move to Cloud SQL.

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:36.650
Now I'm putting Cloud
SQL there in the source.

00:38:36.650 --> 00:38:38.580
And I'm putting Spanner
here in the target.

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:41.160
And all of the sudden, I
can tell a thousand x more

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:42.600
transaction volume.

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:45.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

